The Technology Behind Speech-Driven Facial Animation
Speech-driven facial animation involves coordinating facial motion over time. Mouth movements need to align with speech, the performance needs to remain continuous, and facial details need to convey different expressions.
Research has explored these challenges from several directions. For example, FaceFormer investigates how audio context can be used to generate continuous 3D facial animation, while EmoTalk studies the relationship between speech content, emotion, and facial movement. Together, these approaches illustrate some of the technical challenges involved in speech-driven facial animation.
This preview focuses on the relationship between an emotion setting and the resulting facial expression, using the same audio input across the examples.
Demo Design: One Line, One Audio Clip, Seven Emotions
For this demo, we use the same character image and the same audio clip, then select seven emotion presets: Calm, Angry, Disgust, Fear, Happy, Sad, and Surprise.
The character image and audio remain unchanged; only the emotion setting changes. This makes it easy to compare how the character’s facial expression varies across the seven settings.
